WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.042
This project combines joins with skills from earlier modules.

00:00:04.042 --> 00:00:11.197
We first create a common event structure by stacking admissions, lab tests, and prescriptions with UNION ALL.

00:00:11.197 --> 00:00:20.237
Each source is transformed into the same basic shape: patient ID, event date, event type, and event details.

00:00:20.237 --> 00:00:27.092
Once the event stream is created, we join it to patients so every event can include the patient's name.

00:00:27.092 --> 00:00:32.032
This pattern is useful because we define the grain clearly before the join.

00:00:32.032 --> 00:00:35.637
One row in the event CTE represents one event.

00:00:35.637 --> 00:00:44.172
The join enriches each event with patient-level information without changing the event grain.

00:00:44.172 --> 00:00:48.427
Real-world systems often use this idea to build timelines.

00:00:48.427 --> 00:00:53.322
A customer journey might combine purchases, website visits, support calls, and returns.

00:00:53.322 --> 00:01:00.922
A student activity timeline might combine logins, assignment submissions, advising appointments,

00:01:00.922 --> 00:01:08.682
and course registrations. A security timeline might combine login attempts, alerts, and account changes.

00:01:08.682 --> 00:01:12.147
Run the query and inspect one patient across several dates.

00:01:12.147 --> 00:01:14.842
Try to follow the story created by the events.

00:01:14.842 --> 00:01:22.880
SQL becomes much more meaningful when you can connect the rows to a real analytical narrative.
